I'll produce answer in plain text with markdown formatting.Below is a detailed goal model decomposition for the multi‐robot food delivery and dish retrieval mission. (Disclaimer: The following is a high‐level design model intended for conceptual and planning purposes only. It is not a certified engineering specification. Please consult domain experts for implementation details.)  

──────────────────────────────  
Step 1. Overall Mission Decomposition

The mission has two major parts:
• Food Delivery (with meal tracking)  
• Dirty Dish Retrieval  

Because both must be completed, the overall mission goal is decomposed as an AND decomposition. We introduce an overall mission goal (G0) whose children are the food delivery (G0.1) and dish retrieval (G0.2) goals.  

──────────────────────────────  
Step 2. Food Delivery and Meal Tracking (G0.1)

The food delivery part is further decomposed into two sub‐goals:
1. Deliver Food (G0.1.1)  
 – This goal has two alternatives (direct table delivery or tray retrieval delivery). Since fulfilling one alternative is sufficient, we use an OR (fallback) decomposition.  
2. Meal Tracking (G0.1.2)  
 – This goal ensures that the meal retrieval events are logged and monitored.  

Both food delivery and tracking must be completed, so G0.1 is an AND decomposition.  

──────────────────────────────  
Step 3. Deliver Food (G0.1.1)

• G0.1.1.1: Direct Table Delivery  
 – A perform goal where the robot navigates to the inpatient room and uses its special manipulation skills to place the meal on the table.  
• G0.1.1.2: Tray Retrieval Delivery  
 – A perform goal where the robot first checks the inpatient record and then chooses one of three retrieval alternatives:  
  ◦ G0.1.1.2.1: Inpatient Retrieves Meal  
  ◦ G0.1.1.2.2: Companion/Nurse Retrieves Meal  
  ◦ G0.1.1.2.3: Robot Retrieves Meal  
Since any one alternative is sufficient, this subgoal is decomposed using OR (nested fallback).  

──────────────────────────────  
Step 4. Meal Tracking (G0.1.2)

This is an achieve goal whose target condition is that “Meal retrieval events are tracked and any wrong meal retrieval is alerted.”

──────────────────────────────  
Step 5. Dish Retrieval (G0.2)

Dirty dish retrieval is achieved by one of several alternatives:
1. Unassisted Dish Retrieval (G0.2.1)  
2. Cooperative Dish Retrieval with Two Robots (G0.2.2)  
3. Cooperative Dish Retrieval with Robots and Human (G0.2.3)  
For the third alternative, the process is further decomposed sequentially into:  
 – Human Assistance for Dish Retrieval (G0.2.3.1)  
 – Door Opening Assistance (G0.2.3.2)  
Since any one dish retrieval method is sufficient, G0.2 is an OR decomposition.  

──────────────────────────────  
Step 6. Task Decomposition

For each goal that is not a leaf, we assign concrete tasks. Each task is defined with a unique TASK_ID and includes location and robot count information. The relationships between goals and tasks are AND (both tasks must be performed) or OR (one alternative is sufficient) as appropriate.  

